    "body": "### Contributing guidelines\n\n- [X] I have read [CONTRIBUTING.md](https://github.com/echasnovski/mini.nvim/blob/main/CONTRIBUTING.md)\n- [X] I have read [CODE_OF_CONDUCT.md](https://github.com/echasnovski/mini.nvim/blob/main/CODE_OF_CONDUCT.md)\n- [X] I have updated 'mini.nvim' to latest version\n\n### Module(s)\n\nmini.completion\n\n### Description\n\nAt first I thought this was applicable to all languages and projects, but I was able to trigger two-step completion with `.` just fine in a TypeScript project. So this seems to apply to some languages and not others.\r\n\r\nI spend most of my time in Rust projects where I have to wrestle with this issue. Oftentimes I'd like the completion window to pop up after typing `.` to see all of the methods available for a certain object. But when using `rust-analyzer`, this doesn't seem to work as expected.\n\n### Neovim version\n\nv0.10.1(Build type: Release)\n\n### Steps to reproduce\n\nHave `mini.completion` enabled and an LSP setup with `rust-analyzer`. (I can return with a minimal.lua if needed!)\r\n\r\nFor a minimal Rust project to test this, starting with a Rust toolchain:\r\n\r\n1. `cargo new mini-test`\r\n2. `cd mini-test`\r\n3. `nvim src/main.rs`\r\n4. You can replace the contents of main.rs with the following:\r\n\r\n```rust\r\nfn main() {\r\n    let foo = String::from(\"Hello, world!\");\r\n}\r\n```\r\n\r\nTest autocompletion on `foo`.\r\n\r\n1. On a new line below `let foo = ...`, type `foo.`.\r\n2. Observe that two-step completion isn't triggered, or that we immediately fallback to simple completion.\r\n3. Type any alphabetical character, like `p`. \r\n    a. If fallback completion was triggered before, use the hotkey to manually trigger two-step completion. It should suggest LSP completions as intended.\r\n    b. If fallback completion was not triggered before, two-step completion should have been triggered automatically and should show LSP completions as intended.\r\n\r\nYou can also observe this behavior while trying to autocomplete modules.\r\n\r\n1. On any new line, type `std::`\r\n2. We should expect suggestions to show, but they don't. Repeat steps 2 and 3 from above.\n\n### Expected behavior\n\nTwo-stage completion should be triggered after `.` is typed, so I can scroll through all available methods, fields, etc.\r\n\r\nExample expected behavior in a JavaScript project: \r\n![20240918-131319-s](https://github.com/user-attachments/assets/f486304a-765f-44c1-83de-8b5f92746ac6)\r\n\n\n### Actual behavior\n\nTwo-stage completion is not triggered, and the completion menu does not appear. I have to type another character for suggestions to appear.\r\n\r\nIn this Rust project, fallback completion seems to be triggered instead. (Sometimes it is, sometimes nothing is triggered at all.) Pressing <kbd>C-Space</kbd> to trigger two-step completion manually still falls back to basic completion.\r\n\r\n![image](https://github.com/user-attachments/assets/2096d4c3-aca5-4a73-b214-0c6d0d3bd35e)\r\n\r\nApparently, this same behavior occurs when trying to autocomplete modules (trigger character `:`):\r\n\r\n![image](https://github.com/user-attachments/assets/1fb0e67d-adb9-416b-9cb9-ee91cc3caa58)\r\n\r\nbut after typing a character, two-stage completion can be manually triggered as expected:\r\n\r\n![image](https://github.com/user-attachments/assets/1781dfba-5cc2-42f9-89aa-168b9f1ca8ca)\r\n\r\n![image](https://github.com/user-attachments/assets/4d697691-8ef7-4f94-9077-0818a59a8831)",
